l distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nAlpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma) vs Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumor with Non-Classic Hormonal Dysfunction:\n  Favours first:  abnormal abdomen morphology, abnormal blistering of the skin, abnormal gastrointestinal motility, abnormality of the thyroid gland, acanthocytosis\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nRelated to Hypercortisolism (Cushing Syndrome): acth-dependent cushing syndrome, acth-independent cushing syndrome, adrenal gland disease\n\nRelated to Beta Cell Destruction (Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus): autoimmune disease of endocrine system, autoimmune polyendocrine, diabetes mellitus (disease)\n\nRelated to Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma): pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor\n\nRelated to Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumor with Non-Classic Hormonal Dysfunction: non-functioning endocrine neoplasm, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor, pancreatic non-functioning delta cell tumor\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: Cushing Syndrome: Diagnostic Workup and Imaging Features, With Clinical and Pathologic Correlation. > Treatment / Management\n  In patients with an unknown underlying etiology of hypercortisolism, modulation of hypercortisolism and its complications (such as management of hyperglycemia, diabetes mellitus, hypertension, osteopo\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.66):]\n  Source: Cushing Syndrome: Diagnostic Workup and Imaging Features, With Clinical and Pathologic Correlation. > History and Physical\n  Ultimately, hypercortisolism is associated with considerable morbidity and mortality risk, necessitating early recognition and appropriate management. Hypercortisolism is associated with excess cardio\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Cushing Syndrome: Diagnostic Workup